flags={}
def flag(n,c,msg):flags.setdefault(f'W05-L23-Q{n:03}',[]).append((c,msg))
flag(3,'distractors','The gravity-column alternative is not a plausible mass-matrix property, and skew symmetry combined with zero kinetic energy makes a second alternative immediately self-disqualifying. Use realistic matrix-property confusions, such as symmetric positive semidefinite versus definite under these coordinate assumptions, symmetric but constant, or positive definite without symmetry. Retain one uniquely correct description.')
flag(4,'distractors','The alternatives explicitly announce their own faults with “silently ... without explanation”, “although positive ... assist”, and “with no actuator input even while the motor drives”. Replace editorial self-disqualification with parallel candidate equations and leave their sign/channel errors for the solution to explain.')
flag(7,'distractors','Rotor inertia proportional to electrical resistance and forced zero current despite sufficient voltage are implausible alternatives. Use credible current-versus-voltage regulation confusions, such as unchanged required voltage, increased torque at the same regulated current, or the fixed-voltage current reduction incorrectly carried into this regulated case.')
flag(10,'distractors','Inferring a new physical link mass solely from joint angle is not a credible model-selection action, while declaring the model exact merely because it is linear is overly categorical. Use realistic alternatives such as retaining the frozen linearization without testing its region, updating only gravity, or treating a trajectory-dependent linearization as time invariant.')
flag(13,'assumptions','The stem calls q x^3 a restoring-force magnitude while permitting signed x. For negative x and positive q, q x^3 is negative and cannot be a magnitude; the next graph question explicitly uses (-2,-8). Call it a signed constitutive/resisting term, or restrict the magnitude statement to x>=0 and state q>0.')
flag(13,'explanation','The formula variable definition repeats the contradiction “restoring-force magnitude with signed displacement”. Distinguish signed f_s=q x^3, physical spring force -q x^3, and nonnegative magnitude q|x|^3. The coefficient units and tangent stiffness of the signed term are otherwise correct.')
flag(24,'distractors','Returning +1 and -1 simultaneously as an ordinary scalar and erasing the entire nonzero-speed friction law are weak alternatives. Use plausible zero-speed interpretations, for example adopting a one-sided sign convention, replacing zero with an explicitly bounded stiction interval without changing the solver, or confusing zero velocity with absent friction in the physical model.')
flag(31,'sourceSupport','The gravity question is correctly supported by pages 712–713, but its attached formula record additionally introduces the full desired-acceleration/servo-gain torque law first shown on page 714. Add that exact premise citation, or limit the formula record to the plant/gravity relation actually needed here.')
flag(36,'distractors','Multiplying by gravitational torque then dividing by friction has no plausible basis in the shown partition. Replace o3 with a realistic block-order or inertia-normalization mistake, such as multiplying both the normalized servo and beta by inertia, or dividing the servo by inertia before adding beta.')
flag(38,'distractors','The wrong parameter tuples accumulate unrelated impossibilities: servo gains become link lengths, both friction coefficients become desired accelerations, and all quantities acquire the same units. Use parallel tuples with one realistic distinction per alternative, such as swapping viscous/Coulomb roles or confusing physical and normalized gain units.')
flag(41,'sourceSupport','The solution explicitly relies on the source converting the desired angle with deg2rad, but that statement is printed on page 716, not the sole cited page 717. Add page 716 so the claimed source evidence and the np.cos call are both exactly traceable.')
flag(44,'distractors','A maximum motor torque of 1e-8 N m is not a plausible interpretation of solver tolerance arguments. Replace o4 with a genuine numerical misconception, such as treating rtol and atol as global error guarantees, requiring exactly 1e-8-second time steps, or interpreting them as identical relative-only bounds.')
flag(45,'distractors','Replacing gravity because np.cos cannot accept a scalar is an unrelated false API claim, and a mandatory animation callback does not credibly complete the integrator setup. Use plausible missing-context sets, such as supplying the horizon but omitting x0, defining t_eval outside the interval, or omitting the needed imports.')
flag(48,'sourceSupport','The sole citation is the response plot on page 719, which does not state the homogeneous differential equation or the gains producing it. Add the exact error-dynamics and parameter premises (pages 715–716), retain the explicit initial-condition premise, and identify the closed form as a derivation rather than something read from the plot.')
flag(49,'sourceSupport','The derivative calculation is correct, but page 719 alone shows plotted error/error derivative and does not supply the asserted analytic actual-speed formula. Add the matched error-dynamics, gains and constant-reference premises (pages 715–716; page 717 if using the implemented law), clearly marking the speed formula and peak as derived.')
flag(54,'distractors','The three alternatives test whether a static screenshot magically includes missing callbacks, executes this study app, or proves a hardware run. These are not meaningful robotics distractors. Preserve the missing-animation-code limitation in the source audit, and replace this item with an actual source-bound phase/geometry/control interpretation or an explicitly specified animation-coordinate calculation.')
flag(56,'sourceSupport','Page 721 says that manipulator equations are coupled but does not explicitly show off-diagonal inertia entries or their matrix-product interpretation. Add page 698 for M times acceleration and/or page 722 for the full non-diagonal partition, and label the cross-coordinate interpretation as a derived premise rather than an explicit statement on page 721.')
flag(56,'distractors','An off-diagonal entry implying more physical joints than generalized coordinates is unrelated to the coupling concept. Replace o4 with a realistic confusion, such as identifying it as gravity coupling, a velocity-product coefficient, or only sensor cross-talk.')
flag(59,'explanation','The o4 rationale says [3.5,8] comes from changing the second acceleration from -2 to +2, but that operation gives M[1,2]+beta=[4.5,8], not [3.5,8]. Either change the distractor to [4.5,8], or explicitly explain both the acceleration-sign reversal and subtraction of beta_1 needed to produce [3.5,8]. The correct [0.5,-4] key is unchanged.')
flag(59,'calculations','Independently multiplying M[1,2]+beta gives [2+2+0.5,1+6+1]=[4.5,8]. The numerical mechanism assigned to o4 does not reproduce its displayed first component.')
